![](/images/articles/hero-1.jpeg)

Startups need heroes. They rely on a few people with extraordinary skill and superhuman commitment.

But hero capacity is finite. And hero-driven performance has an incredibly sharp decay curve.

Consider this: Superman (or Wonder Woman) can foil 5 crimes per day. As long as you keep the crime rate under 5 per day, you have no “net” crime. But when you have 6, 7, 8 crimes — the total crime rate appears to rise at extraordinary speed.

Or this: You build a dam with a capacity of 40,000 ML (million liters). The dam works perfectly at 10,000 ML. Flow doubles to 20,000, and doubles again to 40,000, and the dam continues to work flawlessly. But add just 1% more water, and the dam fails catastrophically.

A startup is just like this. We have capacity to handle growth, and we handle it just fine — right up until the day we don’t. And the consequences hit us far faster than we imagine.

See if this story is familiar:

Jane and Jack are the customer success heroes on our team. They have been with us since the beginning, and they know the product inside and out, having been developers before moving into customer success. Jack’s customers tell the CEO how much they love Jack and how they’d never have met their goals without him. Jane was honored as the company MVP for making a customer so successful that they tripled their annual spend.

Jack and Jane can handle ten customers each, but we just sold our twenty-first customer.

We hire Sally. We don’t have a great onboarding program, but Sally shadows Jack and Jane for a few weeks and she is a quick study. She can’t handle ten challenging customers like Jack or Jane, but she can handle seven average ones, and that meets our needs.

Our customer satisfaction is through the roof. We boast 100% customer retention and a net promoter score of 60! What a product we have — what an amazing business!

We continue to grow. We hire two more people. One was recommended by a friend. The second is local; she was a great engineer who wants to be more customer facing. But this time it’s harder to onboard them.

A key project falls behind, the customer complains directly to the CEO, and the CEO puts Jane on a plane. After two weeks of Jane’s 80-hour-a-week efforts, the project is back on track. Jane is a hero again!

Two quarters later, things are different. Our new hires are a mixed bag. Jack and Jane are redirected when we have high value customers or customers in crisis, which occurs with alarming frequency. They’ve been fighting fires, neglecting their long-time customers.

Our customer sat is in free-fall. We knew we wouldn’t stay at an NPS of 60 forever, but how did we fall to 20 so fast? Shouldn’t it have been a steady decline we could have seen coming? Shouldn’t we have had the room to correct the problem before it became catastrophic? What happened?

We exhausted the hero capacity.

Your growing business needs a different kind of hero — the hero who can translate heroic actions into scale. That topic is for my next post. In the meantime, I'd love to hear from you, and learn how you have handled scaling past the hero stage!
